  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWe note that:\n\n- The numerator of $h(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $4.$\n\n- The denominator of $h(x,y)$ is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $3.$\n\nTherefore, the function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $4-3=1.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nA function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $n$ if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)={t}^{n}h(x,y).\n$$\n\nSo first, we compute $h(tx,ty),$ and then we simplify by writing it as ${t}^{n}h(x,y)$ for some $n$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(tx)(ty)^{3}+4(ty)^{4}}{2(ty)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}x{y}^{3}+4{t}^{4}{y}^{4}}{2{t}^{3}{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}(x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4})}{{t}^{3}(2{y}^{3})} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4}}{2{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\frac{x{y}^{3}+4{y}^{4}}{2{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =th(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(tx,ty)=th(x,y)={t}^{1}h(x,y)\n$$\n\nand so the function $h(x,y)$ is homogeneous of degree $1.$",
